# RV1106 kernel defconfig for the duco-master miner head on a ducoCarrier_r0.
#
# The rule here is: this board does exactly one job, and every subsystem that
# has no user on it is off. Not "off because it is big" — off because nothing
# on this image opens it. Where that was checked rather than assumed, the check
# is named.
#
# KEPT, and each of these has a caller:
#   I2C          i2c3 on the M0 pinmux, the carrier trunk. Baked into the DTB
#                (see below), chardev for /dev/i2c-3, rk3x for the controller.
#   Ethernet     stmmac + rk630 phy. The master's only link to the pool, and
#                the only way in.
#   MMC/SD       it boots from the SD card.
#   thermal      per-chip load and throttling decisions read it.
#   watchdog     an unattended miner head.
#   cpufreq+OPP+PVTM   the governor is pinned to performance by S10cpu-performance.
#   FIQ debugger UART console   the ONLY console on this image, and the thing
#                that tells you why a boot failed. Do not remove it to save
#                space; the alternative is a card reader and a guess.
#   GPIO_SYSFS   userspace reads the wired-OR presence INT on GPIO2_A3.
#   VENDOR_STORAGE   the MAC address lives there.
#   IKCONFIG_PROC    /proc/config.gz, so the running board can be asked which
#                config it was actually built with instead of being trusted.
#   LEDs         the on-board work LED (GPIO3_C6, declared gpio-leds in
#                rv1106-luckfox-pico-pro-max-ipc.dtsi) driven by the kernel
#                HEARTBEAT trigger. This is the one thing the LED class buys
#                that userspace cannot: a pattern generated in kernel context,
#                so a hung duco-master leaves it blinking and only a dead
#                kernel stops it. A userspace-driven LED freezes in whatever
#                state it was, which looks identical to a working board.
#
#                The trigger is HEARTBEAT and not the ACTIVITY the vendor dtsi
#                asks for, and the reason is this workload: the miner pegs the
#                CPU by design, and ledtrig-activity blinks at a rate
#                proportional to load — so at 100% it is effectively solid on,
#                indistinguishable from default-state="on" and from a crash.
#                Heartbeat's double-flash stays readable at full load.
#
#                Status LEDs on the carrier are a different job and need none
#                of this: they carry application meaning (pool up, chips
#                present, reject rate), so the master drives them as plain
#                GPIOs through GPIO_SYSFS.
#   ext4, tmpfs, TCP BBR.
#
# STRIPPED, with the reason it has no user:
#   MODULES      the build produces ZERO .ko files. The module loader was
#                infrastructure for nothing.
#   RTC          the whole class, not just the Rockchip driver. The CR2032 on
#                VBAT was removed from the board on 2026-08-21 and the head
#                keeps time by NTP alone, so there is nothing to read at boot
#                and nothing to write back. Its two users went with it:
#                S99rtcinit (which set 2024-01-01 on every 1969 it saw — and
#                with no battery it would have seen 1969 every boot) and
#                ntpd-hook.sh's `hwclock -wu`. CONFIG_SUSPEND is already off,
#                so no wakeup path wanted an alarm either.
#                What makes this safe: S49ntpd runs `ntpd -N` with no `-x`, and
#                busybox ntpd STEPS on its first good reply, so an offset of
#                decades is corrected at once rather than never.
#   all of USB   host and gadget. Nothing composes a gadget on this image —
#                rndis.sh is in the SDK but is not referenced by this
#                BoardConfig and is not in the built rootfs — so usb0 never
#                appears and CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS had no caller. Note the USB
#                maskrom flashing path is NOT lost: that is BootROM, not Linux.
#   CONFIGFS_FS  its only user was USB_CONFIGFS, which is now gone.
#   SPI + SFC    the on-board SPI NAND is a fallback BOOT medium, not something
#                Linux mounts; without MTD/UBI (already stripped) the SFC could
#                not expose it anyway.
#   PWM          checked against the built DTB: no pwm node is status okay and
#                there is no pwm-regulator. The eight PWM clocks this board was
#                once going to generate were replaced by an Si5351A on the I2C
#                trunk.
#   IIO/SARADC   no user; the node is disabled in the DTS to match.
#   I2C_GPIO     bit-banged I2C. The trunk is the hardware controller.
#   VFAT + NLS   no FAT partition exists: env/idblock/uboot/boot/userdata/rootfs,
#                and the last two are ext4.
#   CMA          nothing reserves from it once camera/DRM/NPU are gone, and it
#                was already sized 0 MB.
#   ROCKCHIP_AMP the RV1106's second (RISC-V) core is unused.
#
# DEVICE TREE OVERLAYS ARE OFF, and that is the load-bearing part of this file.
# The peripheral set is fixed by the carrier's copper — one I2C controller — so
# it is compiled into the base DTB instead of applied at runtime.
#
# What that removed is not three symbols. luckfox-config applies pin config by
# dd-writing the boot partition's FIT, and S99luckfoxconfigload ran it at EVERY
# boot: flash wear on the partition that makes the board bootable, plus a
# corruption window if power drops mid-dd. Both that init and
# S99luckfoxcustomoverlay (which would have done the same on SHUTDOWN the moment
# /mnt/cfg/dtbo existed) are out of the rootfs overlay.
#
# DTC_SYMBOLS is what actually disarms luckfox-config: fdtoverlay needs
# __symbols__ in the target to resolve &i2c3, so without it the tool cannot
# apply anything even by hand. Dropping it also halved the DTB (72659 -> 36339 B),
# because dtc -@ assigns a phandle to every labelled node so an overlay could
# target it — 348 phandles became 101.
#
# The cost, accepted: changing a pin needs a kernel rebuild and an SD rewrite
# rather than a menu entry.

